Agentic Code Reviewer opencode

💡 **SUGGESTION:** `normalizeSkillId` is a no-op: both branches of the ternary return the same value (`s`), so the function neither strips a `ws-` prefix nor transforms anything. It reads as if it was intended to canonicalize a `ws-` prefixed id but the implementation was left incomplete. The current behavior is accidentally correct only because the single call site (`listSkillDirs`, line 378) performs its own prefix-tolerant matching (`n === want || n === ws-${want}`). The dead function misleads maintainers into thinking id normalization happens here and invites future callers to rely on it.

**Correção sugerida:**

```diff
 function normalizeSkillId(id) {
   const s = String(id).trim();
   if (!s) return s;
-  return s.startsWith('ws-') ? s : s;
+  return s.startsWith('ws-') ? s : `ws-${s}`;
 }
```

If the `ws-` prefix is intentionally never needed, delete the function and inline the trim at the call site instead.

<details>
<summary>🔍 Detalhes da Análise IA</summary>

**Score:** 3/10 | **Ação dev:** fix-code

**Análise:**
1. Evidence: `doctor.js:385-389` — `function normalizeSkillId(id) { const s = String(id).trim(); if (!s) return s; return s.startsWith('ws-') ? s : s; }` — both ternary arms return `s`. Only call site is `listSkillDirs` line 378, which already handles both `ws-` and bare forms. 2. Scenario: a future edit adds a new caller that uses `normalizeSkillId` expecting a canonical id (e.g., stripping `ws-`), silently receiving the raw input and mis-resolving a skill folder. 3. Protection: no unit test exercises `normalizeSkillId` directly; the smoke tests only assert CLI-level behavior, so the no-op is not caught. 4. Discards: considered that it is harmless today — true, but it is dead/incomplete code in a new 1,154-line file and the function's name promises behavior it does not deliver.

**Caminhos analisados:** /.agents/skills/ws-doctor/scripts/doctor.js
</details>

Agentic Code Reviewer opencode

⚠️ **WARNING:** When a citation is expanded from a path token, its resolution base is wrong whenever `{skillsRoot}` resolves outside the project directory. `buildTokenMap` stores `skillsRoot` as a path *relative to the project root* (`path.relative(projectRoot, skillsRootAbs)`), which for an external or absolute `pathTokens.skillsRoot` override (or a global install) produces `../...`-prefixed values. `expandTokens` substitutes those into citations, and `resolveCitedPath` then falls into the final `else` branch, which resolves the candidate against `path.dirname(sourceFile)` instead of `projectRoot`. So a recipe like `node {skillsRoot}/ws-doctor/scripts/doctor.js` inside a skill body gets resolved under the citing file's own directory, producing a bogus non-existent path. The result is that in the exact hybrid/external-root mode the skill advertises (`resolve {skillsRoot} independently`, absolute `pathTokens.skillsRoot`), the doctor reports false path errors — including against its own SKILL.md — corrupting its primary output.

**Correção sugerida:**

```js
function resolveCitedPath(cited, sourceFile, projectRoot, tokenMap) {
  // Token-expanded citations are project-root relative; plain markdown links stay file-relative.
  const hadToken = BRACE_TOKEN_RE.test(cited);
  // ...existing expansion/normalization...
  } else if (hadToken) {
    abs = path.resolve(projectRoot, candidate);
  } else {
    // Relative to citing file (markdown companion links)
    abs = path.resolve(path.dirname(sourceFile), candidate);
  }
  // ...
}
```

<details>
<summary>🔍 Detalhes da Análise IA</summary>

**Score:** 6/10 | **Ação dev:** fix-code

**Análise:**
1. Evidence: buildTokenMap line 178 stores `skillsRoot: toPosix(path.relative(projectRoot, skillsRootAbs) || ...)`; expandTokens lines 197-213 substitutes tokens; resolveCitedPath lines 307-362 resolves non-`.agents/`/`ws-`/known-prefix candidates at lines 356-357 against `path.dirname(sourceFile)`; the `.agents/`/`ws-` prefix branch (lines 340-355) only covers internal layouts; ws-doctor SKILL.md line 63 uses `{skillsRoot}/ws-doctor/scripts/doctor.js`. 2. Scenario: consumer config sets `pathTokens.skillsRoot` to an external absolute path (or uses a global install); doctor scans that tree; every `{skillsRoot}/ws-*/...` citation inside skill bodies expands to a `../...` path and is resolved from the source file's directory instead of the project root → all reported as missing. 3. Protection: all current smoke tests run in the upstream repo where skillsRoot is internal (`.agents/skills`), so the `.agents/` branch hides the defect; no test covers an external/absolute skillsRoot. 4. Discards: plain markdown `../` links (e.g. `../ws-check-harness/SKILL.md`) are genuinely file-relative and must keep the `path.dirname(sourceFile)` base — so the fix must not change the base for all candidates, only for token-expanded ones.

**Caminhos analisados:** /.agents/skills/ws-doctor/scripts/doctor.js, /.agents/skills/ws-doctor/SKILL.md, /.agents/skills/ws-shared/config-resolution.md
</details>

Agentic Code Reviewer opencode

💡 **SUGGESTION:** draftIssueBody defaults the issue target to the hardcoded string 'jpolvora/workflow-skills' and never reads the dependency graph, while both ws-audit/SKILL.md and AUDIT-FORMAT.md document that the draft targets skill-dependencies.json → upstream.repo. The draft-issue recipe in SKILL.md does not pass --upstream, so the documented resolution path is unreachable through the prescribed flow. For a consumer who installs the package from a fork (npx github:<fork>/workflow-skills), every audit-issue draft would be misrouted to the canonical repository instead of the fork's issue tracker - misdirecting exactly the upstream-fix signal this feature exists to produce.

**Correção sugerida:**

```js
// audit_log.js - resolve upstream from the shipped graph, keep the hardcoded fallback
export function draftIssueBody(session, upstream) {
  if (!upstream) {
    try {
      const graphPath = path.join(__dirname, '../../ws-shared/skill-dependencies.json');
      if (fs.existsSync(graphPath)) {
        upstream = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(graphPath, 'utf-8'))?.upstream?.repo;
      }
    } catch {
      /* fall through to default */
    }
  }
  upstream = upstream || 'jpolvora/workflow-skills';
  // ...rest unchanged
}
```

<details>
<summary>🔍 Detalhes da Análise IA</summary>

**Score:** 3/10 | **Ação dev:** fix-code

**Análise:**
1. Evidence: audit_log.js line 197 (upstream = 'jpolvora/workflow-skills' default; no fs read of skill-dependencies.json anywhere in the module), ws-audit/SKILL.md line 76 ('target skill-dependencies.json → upstream.repo (default jpolvora/workflow-skills)'), AUDIT-FORMAT.md ('draftIssueBody builds a GitHub issue title + body for the upstream repo (skill-dependencies.json → upstream.repo)'), SKILL.md line 71 recipe (draft-issue --session only). 2. Scenario: fork-based consumer install changes the effective upstream, or a future package rename updates skill-dependencies.json upstream.repo; the agent follows the documented skill recipe, draft-issue is invoked without --upstream, and the issue body targets the stale hardcoded repo. 3. Protection: test/test-ws-audit.js asserts only the default draft path (title prefix, body contains finding); no test exercises --upstream or graph resolution. 4. Discards: considered that managed skills always ship from jpolvora/workflow-skills so the default matches today's resolved value - true today, but the docs promise graph-driven resolution and the mechanism to honor it (--upstream) is not wired into the recipe, so the mismatch is real.

**Caminhos analisados:** /.agents/skills/ws-audit/scripts/audit_log.js, /.agents/skills/ws-audit/SKILL.md, /.agents/skills/ws-audit/AUDIT-FORMAT.md, /test/test-ws-audit.js
</details>
